description:
We invite applications for a 3-year funded PhD studentship starting in October 2027 under the co-supervision of Professor Paul Lane and Dr Stefania Merlo, as part of the AFRI-CAN project. This PhD studentship is funded by ERC Synergy grant No. 101224449.
The AFRI-CAN project (ERC Synergy grant No. 101224449 - Lead PI Professor Rob Marchant, University of York) explores the interlinked interactions of human systems and mountain ecosystems understood as mountain socioecological systems (MtSES) by combining state-of-the-art modelling, interdisciplinary insights and valuation assessments to co-produce pathways for sustainable mountain futures. Focussed on six contrasting East African mountain settings, the AFRI-CAN project aims to investigate how communities interact with nature, how ecosystems evolve, and which pathways could support sustainable and resilient mountain futures.
Three interlinked work packages examine socioecological systems at three time scales: 'The Past' (
WP1
) will employ palaeoecology, archaeology, anthropology, history, and ecosystem and climate modelling to investigate the period from 6000 years ago to the present; 'The Present' (
WP2
) will employ anthropology, history, ecology, ecological-economics, pedology, remote sensing, ecosystem and climate modelling and participatory approaches to investigate the period 1970-2025; and 'The Future' (
WP3
) will use data from WPs 1 and 2 to co-design future scenarios for with diverse stakeholders 2030, 2063 and 2100. The fourth work package (
WP4
) will apply our new understanding of the drivers of change to other African and global MtSES through co-production of scenarios and pathways of change with diverse stakeholders to inform how MtSES worldwide could navigate future opportunities and challenges.
